# API route conventions

Route handlers are thin HTTP adapters. Business logic lives in `lib/` or `services/`.

## Required structure

```ts
import { auth } from "@/auth";
import { z } from "zod";
import { createTerm } from "@/lib/glossary";

const bodySchema = z.object({
  source: z.string().min(1).max(500),
  scope: z.enum(["shared", "personal"]),
});

export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const session = await auth();
  if (!session?.user?.email) {
    return Response.json({ error: "Unauthorized" }, { status: 401 });
  }

  const parsed = bodySchema.safeParse(await req.json());
  if (!parsed.success) {
    return Response.json({ error: "Invalid input" }, { status: 400 });
  }

  // Authorization check before mutation
  if (parsed.data.scope === "shared" && !isAdmin(session)) {
    return Response.json({ error: "Forbidden" }, { status: 403 });
  }

  const result = await createTerm(parsed.data, session.user.email);
  return Response.json(result, { status: 201 });
}
```

## Rules

- Export only HTTP methods the route implements
- Wrap DB calls in try/catch — return 500 with generic message, log details server-side
- Use correct status codes: 400 validation, 401 unauthenticated, 403 unauthorized, 404 not found, 409 conflict
- No GET endpoints that mutate state
- Paginate list endpoints that can grow unbounded
